#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9e469-7eec-5401-8986-a4974df572b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363233} {:answer/id #uuid "bbffa08b-7606-549f-bc87-1f500316ebd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363234} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab06c1-b10b-5935-bf2d-75749c31b94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363235} {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} {:answer/id #uuid "c5eaeb1b-839d-5f69-b76b-0cdf9c511c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8043045-7145-51b3-822c-f2d744e6043c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95a58504-d1c2-5301-9dd6-5bea44e00bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140903" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be78f97c-a223-5ab6-b494-bf9064deb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133315} {:answer/id #uuid "076084fc-5240-5f13-bc41-f4207752e1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133316} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcf723b-8f97-5504-88a1-7f4a31a1e4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133317} {:answer/id #uuid "32bf15c7-9ae6-5051-b497-8d314f8c95a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133318} {:answer/id #uuid "41151fbb-e388-5e0f-828b-a2e497a4ca4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133319}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af4ec3ba-fc26-5e37-bc3f-2f09063c1d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133314}] :question/id #uuid "b75151e1-73fe-588a-be1a-8c3a00e71cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-95165" :question/problem "Given that $A = QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n2. $Q$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n3. $R$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364601} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8150ed-2bbb-58ff-9b44-a56ebd9adcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364602} {:answer/id #uuid "c064a103-378c-5690-860f-e149d7fc799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364603} {:answer/id #uuid "4915b708-42a8-572b-b6a9-51e3c5b54b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364604} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65c06a-43c4-5f72-8a34-4f1944f04e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364601} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4def03-8d7b-5557-86f1-b0596397f5f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eed5db2-4721-55a5-93e3-3a839fa38279" :question/math-academy-id "q-95940" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=3{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}={q}_{1}+6{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=3+3+6=12.\n$$"}]}
